<header class="nx-hero"><div class="wrap">
<p class="nx-meta"><a href="/notes/">Guides</a><span>AI adoption</span><span>7 min read</span></p>
<h1>88&nbsp;percent of organisations use AI. Almost none run agents at scale.</h1>
<p class="nx-dek">Using AI and running work through it are two different things. The 2026 Stanford AI Index puts a number on the distance between them.</p>
<div class="nx-byline"><span>By <b>Twinthos Research</b></span><span>Published <b><time datetime="2026-10-08">8 October 2026</time></b></span><span>Updated <b><time datetime="2026-10-13">13 October 2026</time></b></span><span>2 sources, all linked</span></div>
</div></header>
<figure class="nx-fig"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.webp 800w, /assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-1600.jpg" srcset="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.jpg 800w, /assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-1600.jpg 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px" width="1600" height="900" alt="A glass office building at dusk, a grid of windows almost all dark with a few lit" fetchpriority="high"></picture><figcaption>Illustration generated for this guide. It is not a photograph of a real building or client.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="wrap nx-layout">
<aside class="nx-toc" aria-label="Contents"><p>In this guide</p><ol><li><a href="#using-ai-is-now-the-default">Using AI is now the default</a></li><li><a href="#agents-are-where-the-gap-opens">Agents are where the gap opens</a></li><li><a href="#interest-is-outrunning-deployment">Interest is outrunning deployment</a></li><li><a href="#what-explains-the-gap">What explains the gap</a></li><li><a href="#what-to-take-from-it">What to take from it</a></li></ol></aside>
<div class="nx-article">
<section class="nx-take" aria-label="Key takeaways"><h2>The short version</h2><ul><li><strong>88%</strong> of surveyed organisations used AI in at least one business function in 2025, up from 78% in 2024 and 55% in 2023.</li><li>AI agents are a different story. Scaled use was in <strong>single digits</strong> across nearly all business functions, and most respondents reported no agent use at all.</li><li>Even in the tech sector, the best-placed functions reach only 21 to 24% scaled agent use.</li><li>Demand is running ahead of deployment: the share of US AI job postings that mention agentic AI rose from 0.69% to 18.90% in a year.</li></ul></section>

<p>"We use AI" has become close to meaningless as a description of a company. Stanford's 2026 AI Index, the most comprehensive annual measure of the field, shows why: almost everyone is in the 88%, and almost no one is doing the harder thing.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a>,<a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="using-ai-is-now-the-default">Using AI is now the default</h2>
<p>The Index draws organisational data from a 2025 McKinsey &amp; Company survey. It found that 88% of respondents' organisations use AI in at least one business function, up from 78% in 2024. More than half use it in three or more functions.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup> Stanford's regional chart puts Europe at 91% and North America at 90%.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-vcols" id="c-adopt" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-adopt-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-adopt-t">Organisations using AI in at least one business function</strong></figcaption><div class="nx-legend"><span class="nx-key hi"><i></i>Share of surveyed organisations</span></div><ul class="nx-vcols n3"><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb dim"><b>55%</b><i style="--h:55.0%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">2023</span></li><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb "><b>78%</b><i style="--h:78.0%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">2024</span></li><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb hi"><b>88%</b><i style="--h:88.0%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">2025</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">Self-reported by survey respondents. Stanford itself describes the data as directional rather than comprehensive.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Stanford HAI, <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_4_economy.pdf" rel="noopener">AI Index 2026, Chapter 4</a>, citing the McKinsey &amp; Company Survey, 2025. 2023 value from Stanford's <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/economy" rel="noopener">economy summary</a> chart.</p></figure>
<p>The population-level numbers show a similar pace. Stanford reports that generative AI reached 53% adoption in three years, faster than the personal computer or the internet.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup> The spread across countries is wide, and it does not follow the investment league tables.</p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-bars" id="c-country" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-country-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-country-t">Generative AI adoption, share of population, second half of 2025</strong></figcaption><ul class="nx-hbars"><li class="nx-row hi"><span class="nx-lab">United Arab Emirates</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:91.4%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">64%</span></li><li class="nx-row hi"><span class="nx-lab">Singapore</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:87.1%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">61%</span></li><li class="nx-row "><span class="nx-lab">United States (ranked 24th)</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:40.4%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">28.3%</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">The United States leads in AI investment and model development, yet ranks 24th on population-level use.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Stanford HAI, AI Index 2026, citing the Microsoft AI Economy Institute. Three of the top 30 economies shown.</p></figure>
<h2 id="agents-are-where-the-gap-opens">Agents are where the gap opens</h2>
<p>An AI agent, in the sense used in these surveys, is software that carries out a multi-step task with some autonomy, rather than answering one question. It is the form of AI that changes how a process runs, not just how fast one person types.</p>
<p>Here the picture is very different. Across most business functions, a majority of respondents reported no agent use at all. Scaled use was in the single digits for nearly all functions. Even in the functions with the most activity, including IT and knowledge management, about two-thirds or more of respondents reported no use.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-bars" id="c-gap" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-gap-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-gap-t">AI in general versus AI agents at scale (2025)</strong></figcaption><ul class="nx-hbars"><li class="nx-row hi"><span class="nx-lab">Use AI in at least one function</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:88.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">88%</span></li><li class="nx-row "><span class="nx-lab">Highest scaled agent use, tech sector (software engineering)</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:24.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">24%</span></li><li class="nx-row dim"><span class="nx-lab">Scaled agent use, nearly all functions</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:9.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">under 10%</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">Stanford reports 'single digits' without a single figure, so the third bar is drawn at 9% as an upper bound.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Stanford HAI, AI Index 2026, Chapter 4, Figures 4.3.1, 4.3.7 and 4.3.8 (McKinsey &amp; Company Survey, 2025).</p></figure>
<p>The tech sector is ahead, and still not far along. Scaled agent use there reached 24% in software engineering, 22% in IT and 21% in service operations.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<blockquote class="nx-pull"><p>Almost everyone is in the 88%. Almost no one is doing the harder thing.</p></blockquote>
<h2 id="interest-is-outrunning-deployment">Interest is outrunning deployment</h2>
<p>One way to see demand moving faster than practice is job postings. Stanford, using Lightcast data, tracks the share of US AI job postings that mention particular skills. In 2024, 0.69% mentioned agentic AI. In 2025, 18.90% did. Postings mentioning "AI agents" rose from 5.98% to 17.39%.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<ul class="nx-stats"><li><strong>0.69%</strong><span>of US AI job postings mentioned agentic AI in 2024</span></li><li><strong>18.90%</strong><span>mentioned it in 2025</span></li><li><strong>27x</strong><span>the 2025 share against 2024, a 2,643% rise on Stanford's figures</span></li></ul>
<p>Employers' expectations are mixed. Asked how AI will change the size of their workforce over the next year, 43% expect little or no change, 32% expect a decrease, 13% an increase, and 12% do not know.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="what-explains-the-gap">What explains the gap</h2>
<p>The data tells us how wide the gap is, not why. Two readings are supported by what Stanford reports, and one is our own.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Scaling is harder than piloting.</strong> The Index notes that, given the demands of integration, larger companies were the most likely to report that their AI programmes had reached a scaling phase.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>Agent technology is new.</strong> Agentic skills barely appeared in job postings a year earlier. Practice follows tooling, and tooling is recent.</li></ul>
<aside class="nx-callout reading"><span class="nx-callout-l">Our reading</span><p>Opening a chat window is easy and needs no one's permission. Letting software run a process, such as chasing a payment or confirming a booking, needs clear rules, access to systems and a decision about who is accountable when it goes wrong. Those are management tasks, not technology tasks, and they explain why adoption headlines run ahead of results.</p></aside>
<h2 id="what-to-take-from-it">What to take from it</h2>
<p>If you are comparing yourself with "everyone else", the useful question is not whether a competitor uses AI. Nearly all of them do. It is whether any repeating process in their business runs without someone remembering to push it. On the evidence, very few do yet, and that is where an advantage is still available.</p>

<section><h2>Common questions</h2><div class="nx-faq"><details><summary>What percentage of companies use AI in 2026?</summary><div class="a"><p>Stanford's 2026 AI Index reports that 88% of surveyed organisations used AI in at least one business function in 2025, up from 78% in 2024. The data comes from a McKinsey &amp; Company survey and is self-reported.</p></div></details><details><summary>How many companies use AI agents?</summary><div class="a"><p>Far fewer. The Index reports that scaled use of AI agents was in single digits for nearly all business functions in 2025, and that in most functions a majority of respondents reported no agent use at all.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the difference between using AI and using AI agents?</summary><div class="a"><p>Using AI usually means a person asking a tool for help with a task. An agent carries out a sequence of steps with some autonomy, such as gathering information, taking an action in another system and reporting back. The second changes how a process runs; the first changes how fast one person works.</p></div></details><details><summary>Which country has the highest AI adoption?</summary><div class="a"><p>Among the figures in the Index, the United Arab Emirates (64%) and Singapore (61%) lead on population-level generative AI adoption. The United States ranks 24th at 28.3%, despite leading in AI investment.</p></div></details></div></section>
<section class="nx-src"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li id="src-1"><a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_4_economy.pdf" rel="noopener">The 2026 AI Index Report, Chapter 4: Economy (PDF)</a> <span>Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI</span><span>Organisational data: McKinsey &amp; Company Survey, 2025. Country data: Microsoft AI Economy Institute. Job postings: Lightcast.</span></li><li id="src-2"><a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/economy" rel="noopener">The 2026 AI Index Report, Economy: top takeaways</a> <span>Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI</span><span>Summary page with regional chart (Europe 91%, North America 90%) and workforce expectations.</span></li></ol></section>
<div class="nx-log"><b>Corrections and updates</b><p>13 October 2026. Rebuilt on Stanford's primary chapter, with the 2023 and 2024 comparison, country figures and job-posting data added. Stanford's summary page gives generative AI use as 70% of organisations; the chapter text says 79% in 2025 and 71% in 2024. We have left that figure out rather than choose between them. The earlier version repeated one sentence twice and cited a secondary source for the Stanford data; both are fixed.</p></div>
</div></div>
</article>
